## 5. USER ROLES AND AUTHORIZATION

Implement server-enforced role-based access control.

### Parent or guardian

Parents should be able to:

* Register and log in.
* Verify their account where appropriate.
* Complete the applicable parental-consent process.
* Create and manage child profiles.
* Configure content restrictions.
* Set viewing schedules and daily screen-time limits.
* Enable or disable permitted social features.
* Enable or disable video creation.
* Review child-created videos awaiting approval.
* Review appropriate activity summaries.
* Block videos and categories.
* Manage account security.
* Request personal-data export or deletion where applicable.
* Review consent and account-security records.

### Child

Children should be able to:

* Enter their parent-managed profile.
* Watch approved videos.
* Scroll vertically through the feed.
* Browse approved categories.
* Search permitted content.
* Pause and resume videos.
* Adjust volume.
* Read captions.
* Like videos when permitted.
* Save favorite videos.
* Participate in permitted educational activities.
* Record or upload videos if the parent has enabled that functionality.
* Save drafts.
* Submit videos for required approval.
* See the status of submitted videos.
* Report inappropriate content.

Children must not be able to change parental restrictions, access parent-only settings, access administrator functions, or publish unapproved videos.

### Approved content creator

Creators should be able to:

* Manage their creator profiles.
* Upload videos.
* Add titles, descriptions, captions, and categories.
* Submit videos for moderation.
* View approval or rejection decisions.
* Request a review of moderation decisions where appropriate.

Creator privileges must not automatically grant administrator access.

### Moderator

Moderators should be able to:

* Review the moderation queue.
* Examine authorized videos.
* Review reports.
* Approve or reject content.
* Record reasons for decisions.
* Remove content where authorized.
* Escalate serious safety concerns.
* View relevant moderation history.

### Administrator

Administrators should be able to:

* Manage permitted account types and roles.
* Manage content categories.
* Manage featured videos.
* Review reports and moderation statistics.
* Suspend or restore accounts according to policy.
* Manage platform settings.
* Review audit logs.
* Configure moderator permissions.

Apply the principle of least privilege.

Enforce every sensitive permission on the backend. Hiding a button in the frontend is not an authorization mechanism.

